@startuml debounce
' Why a button needs debouncing, and why the fix is a decision rather than a
' delay. Grounded in Src/telemetry.c (press_is_settled), Src/core.c
' (core_millis), Inc/telemetry.h (TELEMETRY_DEBOUNCE_MS).

title Debounce: a decision about time, not a pause

skinparam shadowing false

rectangle "The physical problem" as P #ffdddd {
  card "A button is two pieces of metal meeting.\nThey bounce apart several times over\nthe first few milliseconds.\n\n1 __|‾|_|‾‾|___|‾‾‾‾‾‾‾‾\n0 __| |_|  |___|\n   <--- 1 to 20 ms --->\n\nThe pin shows a BURST of edges.\nA processor polling at megahertz\nsees every single one." as Pp
}

rectangle "What it used to be" as O #ffeedd {
  card "busy_wait(120000) in main.c" as Oo
}

rectangle "What it is now" as N #ddffdd {
  card "press_is_settled(state, now_ms)\nin telemetry, pure logic" as Nn
}

P --> O
O --> N : moved

note bottom of Oo
  Counts NOP instructions, not time.
  Three costs:
  1. Blocks everything for 30 ms.
  2. 120000 means 30 ms only at 4 MHz.
     Raise the clock and it silently
     becomes too short to cover bounce.
  3. Lives in main.c, which no host
     test can reach.
end note

note bottom of Nn
  Asks one question: has it been at least
  TELEMETRY_DEBOUNCE_MS since the last
  accepted edge?

  1. Nothing stalls. The loop stays free.
  2. Real milliseconds from SysTick, so a
     clock change cannot break it quietly.
  3. Pure logic, so a test drives it with
     any clock it likes.
end note

note as Sub
  **The comparison is a SUBTRACTION, deliberately**

  now_ms - last_edge_ms >= DEBOUNCE     correct
  now_ms >= last_edge_ms + DEBOUNCE     broken

  The clock wraps every 49 days. The second
  form overflows near the top, the condition
  goes false, and the button stops responding
  until the counter comes round again.
  Unsigned subtraction has no such hole.

  A delay loop could never be asked about this.
  The test drives the clock to UINT32_MAX - 10
  and walks it through the wrap.
end note

note as Clock
  **Why SysTick runs free instead of reloading every 1 ms**

  The obvious design reloads every millisecond
  and reads COUNTFLAG. But that bit only says
  "reached zero at least once since you last
  read it", never how many times.

  uart_send_string blocks about 2.7 ms per press
  at 115200. During that nobody reads the flag,
  so the clock would lose those milliseconds
  silently and run slow.

  Counting ticks from the free-running counter
  makes a long gap add the right number of
  milliseconds back.
end note

@enduml
